    "updated_at": "2025-11-10T22:27:39Z",
    "body": "Expounding on this a bit, with the current push for Declarative Custom Elements, in theory we could have a \"wrapper\" form control for something like a button like this:\n\n```html\n<form action=\"/foo\" method=\"post\">\n <my-button type=\"submit\" name=\"foo\" value=\"bar\">\n   <template shadowrootmode=\"open\">\n     <button type=\"submit\">Submit!</button>\n   </template>\n </my-button>\n</form>\n```\n\nIt feels unexpected that you can create full shadow DOMs / renderings with declarative custom elements + declarative shadow DOM without JavaScript, but if you need to do anything form related, you need JavaScript and can't do progressive enhancement.\n\nI'm proposing there should be a way for that host `<form>` control to get the `name` / `value` of the button within the `DSD` and submit the form. Declarative Shadow DOM and Declarative Custom Elements lose a lot of \"punch\" if it still can't do progressive enhancement with shadow DOM form controls.\n\n(yes there's other glaring issues like `event.submitter` would technically be a shadow dom button which breaks the \"retargeting\" contract, and if its made to be the wrapping `<my-button>`, it breaks the expectation the `submitter` is always a `button` or an `<input type='submit'>`) ",
    "author_association": "NONE",
    "pin": null,
