    "body": "> Another way to position the above question is _at large_ what do you specifically get from leveraging all of:\n>  ```html\n>  <my-button type=\"submit\" name=\"foo\" value=\"bar\">\n>    <template shadowrootmode=\"open\">\n>      <button type=\"submit\">Submit!</button>\n>    </template>\n>  </my-button>\n>  ```\n> \n> as opposed to only:\n> \n> ```html\n> <button type=\"submit\">Submit!</button>\n> ```\n\n\n\nin particular, slots.\n\nYou can't use slots with elements that don't have a shadow DOM. For example, in Web Awesome, for our buttons, we have \"start\" / \"end\" slots for icons.\n\nFor inputs, we have slots for help text, labels, icons, etc.\n\n\n\n> What about this ties it to the `<template>` element (DSD in particular)? It seems like you  could implement FACEs without ShadowDOM, for example reimplementing <input type=hidden> as  a FACE would not require a ShadowDOM. In that way perhaps this speaks more as a use case  for Declarative Custom Element Definitions.\n\n\n\nif you SSR your web component, it would have a reasonable progressive enhancement story if the DSD can be used. Without it, people will deem web components which render native form controls in the shadow dom largely a 2nd class citizen to existing frameworks because it cannot support this use case.\n\n\n> If we were to implement this (regardless of what the HTML syntax would look like), what would the value be for a custom element which never calls setFormValue? For example if the definition never loaded? Is there an intuitive answer here?\n\n\nThe value would be you largely wouldn't have to think about the form \nassociated APIs. You would kind of get it \"for free\" on simple components. (in my opinion)",
    "author_association": "NONE",
